                <h2>How to Allocate Budget During Cold Start of Your Independent Website</h2>
<p>For newly launched independent websites without data accumulation, the core principle for budget allocation is "small steps, fast iteration." It's recommended to put 60% of your budget into Meta ads (Facebook + Instagram), 30% into Google Search ads, and the remaining 10% for testing TikTok or influencer marketing.</p>
<p>Why this distribution? The cold start phase needs rapid accumulation of conversion data to optimize your models. Meta ads have strong audience targeting capabilities, making them suitable for finding your target customers early on. Google Search ads capture users who already have purchase intent—they complement each other.</p>
<p>For specific numbers, the daily budget during cold start should be no less than $500, running continuously for 2-4 weeks to accumulate data before making adjustments. If the budget is too low, the algorithm lacks sufficient samples to learn from and can easily deviate.</p>
<h2>What's the General Budget Ratio Between Facebook and Google Ads</h2>
<p>There's no standard answer—it depends on your product characteristics and customer purchase journey.</p>
<p>Standard products and functional products (like 3C accessories, home goods) suit a higher search ad ratio, potentially reaching over 50% for Google, because users actively search and compare. </p>
<p>Non-standard products and impulse-buy consumer goods (like fashion apparel, personalized gifts) are better suited for Meta ads, where image and video creatives can more effectively stimulate purchase impulses. In this case, Meta can account for 70%-80%.</p>
<p>A practical judgment method: run Meta ads for two weeks first, observe CPM (cost per thousand impressions) and CTR (click-through rate). If CPM is high but conversions are acceptable, it means your category faces intense competition on Meta—you may need to adjust creative direction or lower bids.</p>
<h2>What's the Difference Between Seasonal Peak and Off-Season Budget Strategies</h2>
<p>During peak seasons (like Black Friday, Christmas), budgets need significant increases, but more importantly, you need to adjust the投放节奏 (campaign rhythm).</p>
<p><strong>4-6 weeks before peak season</strong>: Start warming up, gradually increase budget to 1.5-2x the normal level. The goal at this stage is to accumulate creative data and audience foundation, giving the system enough samples to learn.</p>
<p><strong>1-2 weeks before peak season</strong>: Double the budget again to 3-4x normal levels, while launching all main creatives and audience packages. Experience from a utility app: test 3-5 sets of creatives during the two weeks before peak, and by peak week, only keep the 1-2 sets that show positive ROI.</p>
<p><strong>Off-season</strong>: Don't simply cut budgets—instead, optimize the structure. Pull money from underperforming ad groups and concentrate on verified high-efficiency channels. If any channel shows ROAS below 2 for two consecutive weeks, consider pausing or significantly reducing spend.</p>
<h2>How Does Budget Allocation Logic Differ Between Testing and Stabilization Phases</h2>
<p>The principle during the testing phase is "scatter seeds, harvest the best."</p>
<p>During testing, it's recommended to run 5-8 ad groups simultaneously, with a daily budget of $100-200 per group, covering different audiences and creative combinations. After 2 weeks, filter out the 2-3 best performers and eliminate the rest.</p>
<p>During the stabilization phase, it's "concentrate firepower"—put 80% of the budget into the 1-2 ad groups that have proven successful, using the remaining 20% to continue exploring new audiences or creatives. The biggest mistake during stabilization is running too many ad groups simultaneously, which disperses budget and leaves each group without sufficient data volume for algorithm optimization.</p>
<h2>How to Determine if Budget Allocation is Reasonable</h2>
<p>Focus on three core metrics: <strong>ROAS (Return on Ad Spend), CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost), and LTV (Lifetime Value)</strong>.</p>
<p>ROAS is the most direct indicator, but the reasonable range varies by category: generally, an independent website ROAS above 3 is considered healthy; below 2 means either traffic costs are too high or conversion rates are too low, requiring investigation.</p>
<p>CAC should be evaluated in conjunction with LTV. A more scientific approach is calculating the CAC/LTV ratio—if it's below 1:3, acquisition costs are within reasonable range. If your users have high repurchase rates (like beauty or apparel), LTV is typically 3-5x the CAC.</p>
<p>If you find that CAC in a certain channel continues rising while conversion rates remain unchanged, it indicates that channel has entered the red ocean. Consider moving budget to new traffic channels or testing new ad formats.</p>
<h2>Conclusion: Budget Allocation Isn't Static</h2>
<p>There's no one-size-fits-all formula for independent website ad budget allocation. Different development stages, products, and market competition environments all require budget strategy adjustments. The core principle is: stay flexible, continuously test, and let data speak.</p>
<p>When a channel or creative shows positive ROI, increase investment. When an投放 (campaign) consistently loses money, cut losses promptly.</p>
